WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.030
So, this video is part of my sequence in

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.790
the introduction to digital design using

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.230
VHDL.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.550
And I'm going to look at sequence

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.430
machines using the Mealy model approach.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.950
So, in a previous video, I've used the

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.230
Moore model.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.590
Um where the output is a function of the

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.990
state. And of course, we have

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.590
transitions that which depend on the

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.190
current state and the inputs. The Mealy

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.790
model distinguishing feature is that the

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.870
output is a function both of the current

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.870
state and the inputs.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.550
So, we represent these using a state

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.190
diagram with circles representing nodes

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.710
which correspond to specific states. We

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.790
have arcs that that show the transition

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.270
between these states. And the arcs are a

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.030
function of input expressions. So, you

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.350
change between states uh it's a

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.790
synchronous state machine on a clock

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.990
edge

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.230
uh depending on the input. But, we also

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.350
have Mealy outputs as a function of the

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.910
current state. So, just be clear here,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.630
the outputs here are a function of the

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.670
current state shown on the left here

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.270
before we move we change state. And the

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:12.630
input expression.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.270
So, here is a simple Mealy model

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.750
example. Um we have two states, A and B.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.270
Um and we have single bit coming in. So,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.830
that means we're going to have two arcs

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.030
two possible transitions from each state

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.830
depending on whether the input is a zero

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.630
or a one.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.150
Um so, if we look at

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.110
state A, and you'll see that if the

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.230
input is zero, we stay in state A and

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.350
the output stays at zero. If the input

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.990
is a one, we move across to state B.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.150
Uh and the output is also zero. State B,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.190
if the input is once we're on state B,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.670
if the input is zero, we stay in state B

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.790
and output is zero. And if the input is

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.390
a one, we output a one and go back to

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.270
state A. So, what we'll see here is that

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.070
if the input uh

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.470
value changes to a one, we have a state

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.230
transition between

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.870
A and B and we we switch between them.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.110
So, formally these are represented as

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.910
shown here. A Mealy model defines a

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.029
current state, sorry, the next state is

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:11.230
a function of the current inputs and the

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.390
current states. So, on a clock edge

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.150
then, of course, the current state the

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.750
next state becomes the current state,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.350
but that that that change occurs on a

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.510
clock edge. Now, the output of a Mealy

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.030
model is a function of the current

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.310
inputs and the current state.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:25.830
And because the output is a function of

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.150
the inputs, an output an output can

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.430
change as soon as an input changes. It

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.190
doesn't wait for the state change. It

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.191
doesn't wait for the next clock edge.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.990
So, this is how we'd represent the

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.870
logic. Um as before, we I'm using three

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.430
blocks. We have we have a state

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.550
register, which is clocked.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.750
Um and that basically the output of that

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.830
is our current state. It's whatever bits

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.590
we need for the current state

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.949
changes. So, we have logic, which is a

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:56.550
function of the current state on the

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.190
left, and inputs, which may be

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.430
asynchronous.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.550
Um

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.390
and that determines the next state.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.110
That's pure combinational logic. And

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.550
then we look at the outputs. In this

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.030
case, it's that they're a function of

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.990
both the current state and the inputs,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.990
which may be asynchronous.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.229
So, the outputs are what we call

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:17.550
immediate or Mealy outputs, and they can

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.870
change as soon as an input changes, not

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.550
waiting for a clock edge.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.910
Um now, note that that the Mealy outputs

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:26.870
may be asynchronous if the inputs are

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.350
asynchronous. Um you know, so therefore

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.550
they're just functions of the inputs and

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.830
can change immediately.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.390
That means they're they can they're a

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.470
it's a good basis for a reactive circuit

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.470
where you don't want to wait for the

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.110
next clock for the outputs to change.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.550
You need to respond immediately. So, if

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.110
you want a high-speed reactive

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.229
asynchronous logic circuit, this this

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:46.870
where you need to react promptly, this

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.710
might be an appropriate choice.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.070
Uh pure Mealy models, however, uh the

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.110
outputs are asynchronous and that makes

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.630
them not so useful for when designing uh

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.870
general-purpose synchronous circuits.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.630
Problem we've got is, of course, if we

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.750
have asynchronous signals, if they

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.310
transition,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.110
um

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.710
say they're they're an input to a

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.350
flip-flop and it happens to be on the

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.710
just on the clock edge, uh the output's

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.470
undefined. We may get oscillations. Um

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.030
so, it's not not good for synchronous

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.190
circuits.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.030
So, typically in in general-purpose

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.550
synchronous circuits, we need a we need

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.190
synchronous signals. So, we might have

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.990
then have to synchronize the outputs of

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.710
our Mealy model.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.190
And you do that using edge-triggered

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.830
flip-flops.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.430
So, we can uh synchronize either the

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.270
inputs to the Mealy model output

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.230
combinational logic block. So, we could

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.950
synchronize just the external inputs.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.070
Now, of course, we've lost that

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.830
high-speed reactiveness

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.230
because we're now delaying them till the

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.950
clock edge, but that would mean that the

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.430
outputs of the Mealy circuit would be

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.230
synchronous as well.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.790
We could, of course, add the logic at

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.830
the output of the uh Mealy uh logic

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.990
block before we pass it on to subsequent

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.310
synchronous circuits. And in principle,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.790
you could do both. That's a bit of

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.630
overkill and not necessary.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.230
One of the things to bear in mind, these

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.350
these different techniques will give

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.110
different timing.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.710
So, a synchronized Mealy machine,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.670
depending how you do it, does not

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.590
exactly give exactly the same output

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.990
sequence as an unsynchronized machine.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.350
You have to be clear about that and

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.270
aware of it. So, you need to design

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.830
knowing how what's going to happen, how

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.630
you're going to synchronize it.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.470
So, comparison of a Mealy and a Moore

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.790
model. Moore model with an inherently

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.670
stable synchronized outputs is a

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.470
convenient model to and

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.310
and compatible, you know, with with

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.950
synchronous circuits directly.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.590
Mealy models

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.030
generally require fewer states than

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.270
Moore models, and we're going to see

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.070
that in a subsequent example where I

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.830
look at a sequence detector to satisfy a

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.590
specific requirement, and that could be

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.550
a considerable advantage depending on

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:51.830
the task.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.030
However, the fact that it has

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.110
asynchronous outputs, which then require

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.990
synchronizing logic, might be a

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.230
disadvantage. So, there's pros and cons.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.470
Typically, synchronized output Mealy

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.790
models are often the default model used

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.110
in the US, or at least there is this is

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.510
reflected in many USA-based textbooks.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.710
Whereas in the Europe and the UK, we

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.150
typically have Mealy models in the

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.030
textbooks, and then we also use

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.390
asynchronous

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.909
state machine models. Sorry.

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:21.430
Algorithmic state machine models were

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:22.790
often used as a default models.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.030
Algorithmic state machine, which I'm

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.950
covering in a subsequent video, is

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.710
really combining

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.630
Mealy and Moore immediate outputs and

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.390
state outputs in in one diagram.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.550
And I will cover that later.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.110
If you're using some sort of design

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.430
tools, typically they'll

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.510
accommodate both Mealy and Moore and the

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.750
ASM models. So, design tools will work

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.670
with any of these typically.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:47.870
Um so, we're going to start with a

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.230
simple example, which is just a counter.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.230
Um it's a three-bit

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.550
Gray code ring up counter only. There's

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.790
no input. It just on every clock, it

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.470
counts up in a Gray sequence, as shown

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.230
here. So, one bit at a time is changing.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.150
I've got uh three bits. I've got eight

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.070
states. I've I've numbered them. Rather,

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.270
I should really, you know, could give

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.909
them names. We start at state zero, and

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:11.590
you'll see I've drawn an arc, and

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:12.909
there's no condition. There's only the

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:15.070
output written for the arc.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.510
So, an arc without an input is

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.950
unconditional, and obviously it's a

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.590
transition on the next clock edge. So,

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.990
we'll go from zero to one to two.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.270
Now, you'll I've drawn the output on the

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.150
arc. So, bear in mind that output is

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.470
when we're at

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.510
current state zero.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.630
Right? On and unconditioned depending on

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.909
the input. So, that's the output when

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:36.110
this when we're on state zero, and then

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.670
we we go around, we get the output when

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.070
we're on state one, and so on.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:42.310
Now, let's now look at a sequence

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.710
detector. Look at the sequence detector,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:44.950
it's the same one I've I've used in a

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.510
previous video with the Moore model

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:47.630
example.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.950
We're going to have a single

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.510
single bit input.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.030
And uh we're going to detect the

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.470
sequence 01011.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.830
And we have an output called Y, uh which

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.390
we're going to set to one once we have

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.670
fully detected that sequence. Otherwise,

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.470
the output is zero.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:03.870
So, we start with this we have to start

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.950
with some initial arc. This is basically

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.470
what we uh starting point where we

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.630
haven't detected any of the sequence.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:12.390
From every I've drawn an arrow on the

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.070
left. That's just to show that that's

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.070
that's an initial um

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.510
uh state um or it might be the reset

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:20.390
state.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.790
Uh so, with your single input, we're

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:23.190
going to have to have two trans- two

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.110
transitions from every state

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.350
corresponding to either a zero or a one

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.070
input. And if we match

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.709
a bit in the sequence,

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:35.909
we move along to a new state.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:38.029
So, in this example, if we receive a

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:39.070
zero,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.390
right? We've now got which which uh

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.830
that's like the first bit in the

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.030
sequence. We will need a new state B.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.910
The output, so we draw that with an arc

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.910
with a zero on the left-hand side of the

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.430
slash and a zero showing we still

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:52.870
haven't detected the the sequence, so

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.790
it's its output zero. If we detect a

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:55.750
one,

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.190
well, we haven't detected the initial

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.710
bit of a sequence. We're going to sit in

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.150
state A.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.630
So, I've drawn that with the arc with

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.110
the one which curves back onto state A.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.630
And and we can progress along the

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.950
sequence like that.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.110
Um the output is going to sit stay at

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.670
zero until we've detected the whole

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:12.950
sequence.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.710
Uh if we have a match in the sequence,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.829
we move along to a new state. So, as I

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:20.390
said, state B is I've detected a zero,

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.510
state C is I've detected a zero, and

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.750
then I've detected a one, so two bits

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.270
along the sequence.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:27.870
You'll So, that's you can see that in

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.990
this diagram. Note that for state B, if

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.950
we detect zeros, right? That's if we

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.230
detect a zero zero,

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.350
well, that that zero could be the

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.150
beginning of a sequence. So, we're going

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:40.630
to sit at state B. We don't go back to

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.510
the beginning. Right? Um we're sitting

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.670
there and we're going to sit there. We

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:46.790
you know

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.070
um with uh zeros coming in sitting in

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.550
state B until we receive a one. So,

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.550
we've now received 01. We're on to state

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:56.350
C.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:58.070
Um so, typically then the way I would

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.590
write this is if we receive a bit if it

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.590
could if if the bit is a new part in the

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.190
sequence, we move to a new state. If we

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.110
can see that we've got a subset of the

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.350
sequence being detected, um

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.150
we go back to the the state

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.790
corresponding to the appropriate prefix

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.350
signals. In this case, as I said, uh as

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.950
it uh 010. So, if we go to state uh D,

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.510
that's we've detected uh zero. If we got

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.790
a one at this point, we've received

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:25.550
uh

